#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""comment-lint — block a source comment from depending on context the file cannot carry.

WHY THIS EXISTS
    A comment is the only artifact with no reader-facing surface. A document is read by
    someone who wanted it and can notice it is wrong; a comment is read by whoever is
    already editing that line, who is looking at the code and not auditing the prose
    beside it. So a comment that has gone stale produces no complaint from anybody, ever.
    The drift is invisible by construction rather than by neglect — which makes it a gate
    problem and not a care problem.

    The second-order cost is the one that bites later. A comment naming a record from the
    tracker it was written under, or citing a count taken on the day it was typed, does
    not merely age badly: it BINDS the file to the repository it was written in. A tool
    built to be copied elsewhere cannot be, and nobody finds out until the copy is
    attempted.

WHAT IT CHECKS
    program-marker   a record id, tracker key or private tree name, BARE in commentary.
                     The vocabulary is CONFIGURED, never built in — see VOCABULARY below.
    plan-identifier  a reference to a numbered unit of a plan ("Task 8", "Phase 2"). The
                     plan is not in the file, is often not in the repository, and is
                     discarded once the work lands, so the pointer dies where it stands.
    session-deixis   prose that only resolves for whoever was in the room — "this task",
                     "as requested", "at the time of writing".
    personal-path    an absolute path under a named user's home directory.
    stale-hedge      a sentence that announces its own expiry date ("as of this writing").
    stale-count      ADVISORY. A census in prose — "N entries", "as of <date>". A real
                     defect when it is a measurement, and ordinary content when it
                     describes a fixture the file itself defines. See BLOCKING vs ADVISORY.

WHAT IT CANNOT CHECK — stated because a gate that hides its blind spots gets over-trusted
    * Whether a comment explains WHY rather than restating WHAT. That is the primary
      standard and it is a judgement; no pattern sees it.
    * Whether prose is relevant to the project it sits in. Also a judgement, and the
      reason the sibling standard for documentation is enforced by review instead.
    * A record id in a vocabulary nobody configured. With no vocabulary the category is
      INACTIVE, and says so on stderr rather than passing silently.
    * A stale claim with no number in it — "the parser handles both forms" after a third
      form was added. Every word still resolves and the sentence is false.
    * Whether a permitted historical note is TRUE. Provenance is exempted by shape, not
      verified.

MENTION vs USE — the calibration that decides whether this gate survives
    Some files legitimately name the very tokens this checks for, because matching them is
    what the file DOES: a linter resolving a record id, a test asserting on one, a status
    line preferring one path spelling over another. Those strings are behaviour, not prose,
    and removing them breaks the tool.

    The discriminator is quoting. A token wholly inside a backtick span or a double-quoted
    span is being NAMED — it is a literal under discussion. Bare in the sentence, it is
    being RELIED ON. Single quotes are deliberately not span delimiters: an apostrophe in
    ordinary prose would pair with the next one and silently swallow a real finding.

    Without this, every file that implements the standard is a finding against itself.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY PERMITTED
    * Provenance. "On <date> this broke, which is why this check exists" cannot go stale:
      the event happened. "As of <date> there are N" will. The test is one question —
      could an edit made somewhere else, by someone else, later make this sentence false?
    * TODO, FIXME and friends. They are an industry convention with tooling behind them,
      and a standard that fought its own ecosystem would simply be ignored.
    * Step numbering that the file itself defines. "Step 1: ... Step 2: ..." describing an
      algorithm in the function below is self-contained. This was measured, not assumed:
      on the corpus this was built against, that spelling was mostly self-contained prose
      and external-standard citation, so the word is absent from the plan vocabulary. The
      measurement lives in the decision record, not here.

VOCABULARY
    The marker vocabulary is one extended regular expression, read from the first
    non-comment line of a file named by --markers, by $COMMENT_LINT_MARKERS, or
    `.comment-lint-markers` at the repository root, in that order.

    It is configuration rather than a built-in list for a reason that is easy to get
    backwards: a tracker's id scheme is itself private in most of the places this tool is
    meant to run, so a built-in list would make the tool un-installable in exactly the
    repositories that need it, and would be the leak it exists to prevent. The engine
    travels; the vocabulary stays home.

BLOCKING vs ADVISORY
    Decided by precision measured against a real corpus, not by how serious a defect
    sounds. `stale-count` advises rather than blocks: a number beside a noun is a rotting
    census in a module header and an accurate description of its own fixture in a test, and
    no pattern separates those. The rest block.

    Precision is a property of a corpus, so re-measure before changing this split. The
    measurement lives in the decision record — a number pasted into a comment is stale the
    next time anyone edits a file, which is the defect this tool exists to catch.

RATCHET
    `--added-only` reads each file's STAGED BLOB and reports only findings on lines the
    commit ADDS. A blocking gate introduced over a corpus that already violates it would
    refuse edits to long-lived files over defects the committer did not write, and would be
    switched off, after which it guards nothing. Inherited findings surface in a full run
    and are cleaned up deliberately. Reading the staged blob also means a partial `git add`
    cannot slip a defect past this check.

HOW A COMMENT IS FOUND
    Every check over comments needs the same hard part first: deciding which bytes of a
    source file are prose and which are code. A naive grep for a comment marker reads a
    URL, a hash inside a string literal and a here-document body as commentary, and each
    of those is a false positive on content nobody wrote as prose.

    So this is a character scanner with per-language string and comment rules, not a
    parser. It is wrong on genuinely ambiguous syntax — a regex literal containing a
    comment marker is the known case — and right on the shapes that actually occur. A
    file whose language is not in the table yields nothing, so an unknown extension is a
    silent no-op rather than a stream of garbage. Python is handled by the language's own
    tokenizer, plus its syntax tree for docstrings, because a docstring is documentation
    by position and guessing at that is exactly what this avoids.

    Machine-written files are skipped wholesale. A generator's banner is a generator's
    words; flagging it teaches nobody anything and trains the reader to ignore the tool.

USAGE
    comment-lint.py [--blocking-only] [--added-only] [--stats] [--markers FILE] [PATH ...]

    No PATH arguments lints every tracked source file. Exit 1 if any BLOCKING finding.
    Output format matches the repo's other linters: `[category] path:line: message`.
"""
import ast
import io
import os
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import tokenize

# --- language table ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Each flavour names its line-comment tokens, block-comment pairs, and string delimiters.
# A string delimiter carries whether a backslash escapes inside it, because a shell
# single-quoted string has no escapes and a C-like one does — getting that backwards
# swallows the rest of a file at the first apostrophe.
HASH = {
    "line": ["#"],
    "block": [],
    "strings": [("'", "'", False), ('"', '"', True)],
    "heredoc": True,
}
SLASH = {
    "line": ["//"],
    "block": [("/*", "*/")],
    "strings": [("'", "'", True), ('"', '"', True), ("`", "`", True)],
    "heredoc": False,
}
BLOCK_ONLY = {
    "line": [],
    "block": [("/*", "*/")],
    "strings": [('"', '"', True), ("'", "'", True)],
    "heredoc": False,
}
MARKUP = {
    "line": [],
    "block": [("<!--", "-->")],
    "strings": [],
    "heredoc": False,
}
DASH = {
    "line": ["--"],
    "block": [],
    "strings": [("'", "'", False), ('"', '"', True)],
    "heredoc": False,
}

FLAVOURS = {}
for _ext in (
    "py pyi sh bash zsh ksh rb pl pm yaml yml toml just tf tfvars cfg conf ini "
    "service timer socket mount network automount nix ps1 r jl"
).split():
    FLAVOURS[_ext] = HASH
for _ext in (
    "js mjs cjs jsx ts mts cts tsx c h cc cpp hpp rs go java kt kts swift scala "
    "php dart groovy rn proto typ"
).split():
    FLAVOURS[_ext] = SLASH
for _ext in "css".split():
    FLAVOURS[_ext] = BLOCK_ONLY
for _ext in "scss sass less".split():
    FLAVOURS[_ext] = SLASH
for _ext in "html htm xml svg xsl".split():
    FLAVOURS[_ext] = MARKUP
for _ext in "sql lua hs elm".split():
    FLAVOURS[_ext] = DASH

# Extensionless files whose name is the convention. A shebang is checked first, so this
# only has to carry names that are not scripts.
BY_NAME = {
    "justfile": HASH,
    "Justfile": HASH,
    "Makefile": HASH,
    "makefile": HASH,
    "Dockerfile": HASH,
    "Brewfile": HASH,
    "PKGBUILD": HASH,
    ".gitconfig": HASH,
    ".gitignore": HASH,
    ".zshrc": HASH,
    ".bashrc": HASH,
    ".profile": HASH,
}

SHEBANG = re.compile(r"^#!.*\b(sh|bash|zsh|python[0-9.]*|ruby|perl)\b")
HEREDOC_OPEN = re.compile(r"<<-?\s*(['\"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\1")

# Skipped wholesale: machine-written files, where a "comment" is a generator's banner and
# no human chose its words. Flagging those teaches nobody anything and trains the reader
# to ignore the tool.
GENERATED = re.compile(
    r"(^|/)(node_modules|vendor|third[-_]party|dist|build|\.venv|venv)/"
    r"|\.min\.(js|css)$"
    r"|(^|/)[^/]*\.generated\.[^/]+$"
    r"|(^|/)(package-lock\.json|pnpm-lock\.yaml|yarn\.lock|uv\.lock|Cargo\.lock|poetry\.lock)$"
)

# A generator's own banner, wherever it lands. Same reasoning as GENERATED, for a file
# that is checked in beside hand-written ones rather than living in a generated tree.
GENERATED_BANNER = re.compile(
    r"(do not edit|don't edit|auto[- ]?generated|automatically generated|generated by)",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)


def flavour_for(path: str, first_line: str = "") -> dict | None:
    """The scanner rules for this file, or None when the language is unknown."""
    if GENERATED.search(path):
        return None
    name = path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
    if name in BY_NAME:
        return BY_NAME[name]
    if "." in name:
        ext = name.rsplit(".", 1)[-1]
        if ext in FLAVOURS:
            return FLAVOURS[ext]
    if SHEBANG.match(first_line):
        return HASH
    return None


def _scan(text: str, fl: dict) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:
    """Character scanner. Returns (line number, comment text) per line of commentary."""
    out: list[tuple[int, str]] = []
    lines = text.splitlines()
    in_block: tuple[str, str] | None = None
    heredoc_end: str | None = None

    for n, raw in enumerate(lines, 1):
        i = 0
        line = raw

        if heredoc_end is not None:
            if line.strip() == heredoc_end:
                heredoc_end = None
            continue

        # Continue an open block comment: the whole line is prose until the closer.
        if in_block is not None:
            close = in_block[1]
            idx = line.find(close)
            if idx == -1:
                out.append((n, line.strip()))
                continue
            out.append((n, line[:idx].strip()))
            i = idx + len(close)
            in_block = None

        in_string: tuple[str, bool] | None = None
        started: int | None = None
        while i < len(line):
            ch = line[i]
            if in_string is not None:
                close, escapes = in_string
                if escapes and ch == "\\":
                    i += 2
                    continue
                if line.startswith(close, i):
                    in_string = None
                    i += len(close)
                    continue
                i += 1
                continue

            opened = False
            for op, cl, escapes in fl["strings"]:
                if line.startswith(op, i):
                    in_string = (cl, escapes)
                    i += len(op)
                    opened = True
                    break
            if opened:
                continue

            for op, cl in fl["block"]:
                if line.startswith(op, i):
                    rest = line[i + len(op) :]
                    idx = rest.find(cl)
                    if idx == -1:
                        out.append((n, rest.strip()))
                        in_block = (op, cl)
                        i = len(line)
                    else:
                        out.append((n, rest[:idx].strip()))
                        i = i + len(op) + idx + len(cl)
                    break
            else:
                hit = None
                for tok in fl["line"]:
                    if not line.startswith(tok, i):
                        continue
                    # `://` is a URL, not a comment, and it is the single largest false
                    # positive for a slash-flavoured scanner.
                    if tok == "//" and i > 0 and line[i - 1] == ":":
                        continue
                    # A hash starts a comment only at a token boundary. Without this,
                    # `${var#prefix}` and a fragment in a bare URL both read as prose.
                    if tok == "#" and i > 0 and not line[i - 1].isspace():
                        continue
                    hit = tok
                    break
                if hit is not None:
                    started = i + len(hit)
                    break
                i += 1

        if started is not None:
            out.append((n, line[started:].strip()))
            continue

        if fl.get("heredoc"):
            m = HEREDOC_OPEN.search(line)
            if m:
                heredoc_end = m.group(2)

    return [(n, t) for n, t in out if t]


def _python(text: str) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:
    """Python: comments from the tokenizer, docstrings from the syntax tree.

    The tokenizer is used rather than the scanner above because it is the language's own
    answer to which hashes are prose, and it costs nothing to ask. Docstrings need the
    tree: a string is only documentation in certain positions, and telling those apart by
    eye is exactly the kind of guess this module avoids.
    """
    out: list[tuple[int, str]] = []
    try:
        for tok in tokenize.generate_tokens(io.StringIO(text).readline):
            if tok.type == tokenize.COMMENT:
                out.append((tok.start[0], tok.string.lstrip("#").strip()))
    except (tokenize.TokenError, IndentationError, SyntaxError):
        return _scan(text, HASH)

    try:
        tree = ast.parse(text)
    except SyntaxError:
        return sorted(set(out))

    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if not isinstance(
            node, (ast.Module, ast.ClassDef, ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)
        ):
            continue
        body = getattr(node, "body", None)
        if not body:
            continue
        first = body[0]
        if not (
            isinstance(first, ast.Expr)
            and isinstance(first.value, ast.Constant)
            and isinstance(first.value.value, str)
        ):
            continue
        start = first.value.lineno
        for offset, line in enumerate(first.value.value.splitlines()):
            if line.strip():
                out.append((start + offset, line.strip()))

    return sorted(set(out))


def extract(text: str, path: str) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:
    """Every line of commentary in `text`, as (line number, prose)."""
    first = text.split("\n", 1)[0] if text else ""
    fl = flavour_for(path, first)
    if fl is None:
        return []
    name = path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
    if name.endswith((".py", ".pyi")):
        found = _python(text)
    else:
        found = _scan(text, fl)
    if any(GENERATED_BANNER.search(t) for _, t in found[:5]):
        return []
    return found

# A numbered unit of a plan. `Step` is absent by measurement, not by oversight — see
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY PERMITTED.
PLAN_ID = re.compile(
    r"\b(Task|Phase|Wave|Sprint|Milestone|Iteration|Batch)\s+\d+(?:\.\d+)*[a-z]?\b",
)

# Deixis that resolves only for whoever was present. `this session`, `this pass` and
# `currently` are absent deliberately: they are domain vocabulary in session tooling and
# in code describing runtime state, and flagging them there is how a gate loses its
# reader.
DEIXIS = re.compile(
    r"\bthis (task|sitting|conversation|exchange)\b"
    # `as requested` must end its clause. Without that, `one entry per requested
    # strategy` and `as requested by the caller` — ordinary API prose — read as deixis.
    r"|\bas (requested|discussed)(?=\s*[,.;:)]|\s*$)"
    r"|\bper (your|our) (request|conversation|discussion)\b"
    r"|\bthe user (asked|wants|said|requested)\b"
    r"|\byou asked\b|\bwe discussed\b|\bearlier today\b"
    r"|\b(the|this) (task|plan)'s (brief|scope)\b",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)

# An absolute path under a named user's home. The leading boundary is what keeps a
# repo-relative `routes/home/...` out of it.
PERSONAL_PATH = re.compile(r"(?<![\w.-])/(home|Users)/[a-z][\w.-]*/")

# A sentence that announces its own expiry.
STALE_HEDGE = re.compile(
    r"\bat (the time of|this) writing\b|\bas of (this writing|today|now)\b",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)

# ADVISORY. A census in prose. Bounded so a threshold or a limit does not read as one.
CENSUS = re.compile(
    r"(?<![\w.-])(?:~|about |roughly |approximately )?\d+\s+"
    r"(rows|files|findings|violations|entries|packages|projects|posts|URLs|repos|"
    r"machines|hits|gaps|skills|agents|topics|commits|tests|cases)\b",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
CENSUS_NOT = re.compile(
    r"\b(last|first|up to|beyond|at most|at least|fewer than|more than|per)\s+$"
    r"|[<>≥≤]=?\s*$"
)
ASOF_DATE = re.compile(
    r"\bas of\s+(20\d\d|[A-Z][a-z]+ 20\d\d|\d\d?/\d\d?/\d\d)", re.IGNORECASE
)

BACKTICK = re.compile(r"`[^`]*`")
DQUOTE = re.compile(r'"[^"]*"')
HUNK = re.compile(r"^@@ -\d+(?:,\d+)? \+(\d+)(?:,(\d+))? @@")

ADVISORY = {"stale-count"}


def git(*args: str, cwd: pathlib.Path) -> str:
    # Decoded with replacement rather than strictly: a caller may hand this every staged
    # path, including a binary one, and a blocking gate must not die on an image.
    out = subprocess.run(["git", *args], cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, check=True).stdout
    return out.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")


def repo_root(start: pathlib.Path) -> pathlib.Path:
    return pathlib.Path(git("rev-parse", "--show-toplevel", cwd=start).strip())


def quoted_spans(text: str) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:
    return [m.span() for m in BACKTICK.finditer(text)] + [
        m.span() for m in DQUOTE.finditer(text)
    ]


def is_mention(span: tuple[int, int], spans: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> bool:
    return any(a <= span[0] and span[1] <= b for a, b in spans)


def load_markers(root: pathlib.Path, explicit: str | None) -> re.Pattern | None:
    """The configured marker vocabulary, or None when nothing configured one."""
    candidates = [
        explicit,
        os.environ.get("COMMENT_LINT_MARKERS"),
        str(root / ".comment-lint-markers"),
    ]
    for c in candidates:
        if not c:
            continue
        p = pathlib.Path(c)
        if not p.is_file():
            continue
        for line in p.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
            s = line.strip()
            if s and not s.startswith("#"):
                try:
                    return re.compile(s)
                except re.error as e:
                    print(f"comment-lint: {p}: unusable pattern: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
                    return None
    return None


def check_comments(
    comments: list[tuple[int, str]], rel: str, markers: re.Pattern | None
) -> list[tuple[str, str, int, str]]:
    findings = []
    for n, text in comments:
        spans = quoted_spans(text)

        if markers is not None:
            for m in markers.finditer(text):
                if is_mention(m.span(), spans):
                    continue
                findings.append(
                    (
                        "program-marker",
                        rel,
                        n,
                        f"`{m.group(0)}` in commentary — a record id resolves only where "
                        "that tracker lives, so it dies the moment this file is copied "
                        "or the record is retired. Describe the thing in its own terms; "
                        "let the record point at the code, not the code at the record. "
                        "(A token inside backticks or double quotes is read as a named "
                        "literal and is not reported.)",
                    )
                )

        for m in PLAN_ID.finditer(text):
            if is_mention(m.span(), spans):
                continue
            findings.append(
                (
                    "plan-identifier",
                    rel,
                    n,
                    f"`{m.group(0)}` — a numbered unit of a plan that is not in this "
                    "file, is usually not in this repository, and is discarded once the "
                    "work lands. State what the code does and why; a reader who never "
                    "saw the plan is the only reader this comment will ever have.",
                )
            )

        for m in DEIXIS.finditer(text):
            if is_mention(m.span(), spans):
                continue
            findings.append(
                (
                    "session-deixis",
                    rel,
                    n,
                    f"`{m.group(0)}` — resolves only for whoever was present when this "
                    "was written. Name the thing: which behaviour, which constraint, "
                    "which defect.",
                )
            )

        for m in PERSONAL_PATH.finditer(text):
            if is_mention(m.span(), spans):
                continue
            findings.append(
                (
                    "personal-path",
                    rel,
                    n,
                    f"`{m.group(0)}` — an absolute path under one user's home, on one "
                    "machine. Use the environment variable, a repo-relative path, or "
                    "describe where the file belongs.",
                )
            )

        for m in STALE_HEDGE.finditer(text):
            if is_mention(m.span(), spans):
                continue
            findings.append(
                (
                    "stale-hedge",
                    rel,
                    n,
                    f"`{m.group(0)}` — the sentence dates itself and nothing will "
                    "re-date it. Either the claim is durable, in which case drop the "
                    "hedge, or it is a measurement, in which case it belongs where "
                    "measurements are kept.",
                )
            )

        for m in ASOF_DATE.finditer(text):
            if is_mention(m.span(), spans):
                continue
            findings.append(
                (
                    "stale-count",
                    rel,
                    n,
                    f"`{m.group(0)}` — a claim pinned to a date. Provenance for an event "
                    "is fine; a state that has since changed is not.",
                )
            )
        for m in CENSUS.finditer(text):
            if is_mention(m.span(), spans):
                continue
            if CENSUS_NOT.search(text[: m.start()]):
                continue
            findings.append(
                (
                    "stale-count",
                    rel,
                    n,
                    f"`{m.group(0).strip()}` — a census in prose. If it counts something "
                    "this file defines, it is fine; if it counts something elsewhere, an "
                    "edit somewhere else makes it false and nothing will notice.",
                )
            )
    return findings


def added_lines(root: pathlib.Path, rel: str) -> set[int]:
    out = git("diff", "--cached", "-U0", "--", rel, cwd=root)
    added: set[int] = set()
    for line in out.splitlines():
        m = HUNK.match(line)
        if m:
            start = int(m.group(1))
            count = 1 if m.group(2) is None else int(m.group(2))
            added.update(range(start, start + count))
    return added


REMEDY = """
  What to write instead — and what NOT to write instead, which is the failure a first
  repair usually walks into:
    * Replace the pointer with the SUBSTANCE it pointed at. A comment that said which
      record required a behaviour should say what the behaviour is and why it is required.
    * Do NOT swap it for a restatement of the code. "Loop over the items" is a worse
      comment than the one you removed, and it will never be flagged by anything.
    * Do NOT swap a marker for a vaguer gesture at the same absent thing — "per the plan",
      "as decided elsewhere". That is the same dangling pointer with the evidence removed.
    * Do NOT delete a comment carrying real provenance to reach green. "On <date> this
      broke, which is why this check exists" is permitted and is usually the most valuable
      sentence in the file.
"""


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    blocking_only = "--blocking-only" in argv
    added_only = "--added-only" in argv
    stats = "--stats" in argv
    markers_file = None
    rest = []
    it = iter(argv)
    for a in it:
        if a == "--markers":
            markers_file = next(it, None)
        elif a in ("--blocking-only", "--added-only", "--stats"):
            continue
        else:
            rest.append(a)

    start = pathlib.Path.cwd()
    root = repo_root(start)
    markers = load_markers(root, markers_file)
    if markers is None:
        print(
            "comment-lint: no marker vocabulary configured — the program-marker category "
            "is INACTIVE. Point --markers or $COMMENT_LINT_MARKERS at a file whose first "
            "non-comment line is an extended regular expression, or add "
            ".comment-lint-markers at the repository root.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )

    tracked = {p for p in git("ls-files", cwd=root).splitlines() if p}
    if rest:
        targets = []
        for a in rest:
            rel = str((start / pathlib.Path(a)).resolve().relative_to(root))
            if rel in tracked:
                targets.append(rel)
    else:
        targets = sorted(tracked)

    findings = []
    n_files = n_comments = 0
    for rel in targets:
        if added_only:
            try:
                text = git("show", f":{rel}", cwd=root)
            except subprocess.CalledProcessError:
                continue
        else:
            f = root / rel
            if not f.is_file():
                continue
            try:
                text = f.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
            except OSError:
                continue
        first = text.split("\n", 1)[0] if text else ""
        if flavour_for(rel, first) is None:
            continue
        comments = extract(text, rel)
        if not comments:
            continue
        n_files += 1
        n_comments += len(comments)
        hits = check_comments(comments, rel, markers)
        if added_only:
            keep = added_lines(root, rel)
            hits = [h for h in hits if h[2] in keep]
        findings.extend(hits)

    # One line naming the same token twice is one defect to fix, not two. Sorted on the
    # WHOLE record, not on a prefix of it: a set iterates in hash order, so a partial key
    # leaves two findings that share a file, a line and a category to be ordered at
    # random -- and Python salts string hashing per process, so the output changes
    # between runs of the same tool over the same tree. Caught by diffing one version
    # against another and finding the diff was the tool disagreeing with itself.
    findings = sorted(set(findings), key=lambda f: (f[1], f[2], f[0], f[3]))

    if blocking_only:
        findings = [f for f in findings if f[0] not in ADVISORY]
    blocking = [f for f in findings if f[0] not in ADVISORY]

    for cat, rel, n, msg in findings:
        print(f"[{cat}] {rel}:{n}: {msg}")

    if stats:
        counts: dict[str, int] = {}
        for cat, *_ in findings:
            counts[cat] = counts.get(cat, 0) + 1
        print(
            f"\ndenominator: {n_comments} comment lines in {n_files} source files",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        for cat in sorted(counts):
            mark = " (advisory)" if cat in ADVISORY else ""
            print(f"  {cat}: {counts[cat]}{mark}", file=sys.stderr)

    if findings:
        print(REMEDY, file=sys.stderr)
        print(
            f"{len(blocking)} blocking, {len(findings) - len(blocking)} advisory. "
            "What this gate CANNOT check is listed in its module docstring; a green run "
            "is not a comment-quality guarantee.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
    return 1 if blocking else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
